#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172946} :activity/id #uuid "811b6c3b-bd9c-5c41-8fe9-0970ca3fedb9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049433 :topic/id #uuid "f4f8286a-494e-5683-85be-d6899a52bb51" :topic/math-academy-id 3289} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172946 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173179 :tutorial/content "The **gamma function** $\\Gamma(z)$ extends the factorial function to positive real numbers and parts of the complex plane.\n\nIn particular, for any natural number\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(n) = (n-1)\n$$\n\nIf the complex number $z$ satisfies $\\textrm{Re}(z) > 0$, then the gamma function can be defined as the improper integral\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(z) = \\int_0^\\infty t^{z-1} e^{-t} \\, \\textrm dt\n$$\n\nIn this lesson, we will only consider cases where $z$ is a positive real number." :tutorial/id #uuid "9fea375f-e55b-5c47-a7ae-c2ef9f860a2c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5337 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4bee0881-4e23-5850-9acc-e043caf9668c" :step/math-academy-id 33407 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172952}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172947 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172064 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171870 :question/id #uuid "62da71f1-5a3a-509a-a68e-0596f88c4ae4" :question/math-academy-id "e-8951"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "59f9abf1-17db-5717-8660-a531ef00b15c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049341}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172234}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Gamma Function for a Positive Integer"} :step/id #uuid "96bce02d-680a-5dd7-a5f2-34c1e5553c65" :step/math-academy-id 33409 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172948}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172948 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173180 :tutorial/content "To evaluate the gamma function at positive integer multiples of one-half, we use the following recursive formula:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(z+1) = z \\Gamma(z), \\qquad \\Gamma \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) = \\sqrt{\\pi}\n$$\n\nWe'll prove these results at the end of the lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "a2e308ea-2921-5cda-bb43-b4914e8efaf1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5338 :tutorial/title "The Recursive Formula and the Value of the Gamma Function at One-Half"} :step/id #uuid "5e5c30dd-3fe8-5761-b1e6-76ab3ced125d" :step/math-academy-id 33410 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172949}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172949 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172065 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171871 :question/id #uuid "5d084f86-9874-5bdd-a416-dba742d7e4e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-8952"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c8de43f-dc61-5bbe-8b84-b8aa0be19146" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050380}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172235}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Gamma Function at a Multiple of One-Half"} :step/id #uuid "bf112a76-b1a6-5da1-b844-9eb0f13eb3f1" :step/math-academy-id 33411 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173037}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172950 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173181 :tutorial/content "Previously, we used the following relationship between the gamma function and the factorial:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(n) = (n-1)\n$$\n\nThis relationship holds for any natural number\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nNow, let's prove it.\n\nFirst, we start with the following identity, which we proved earlier:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(z+1) = z \\Gamma(z)\n$$\n\nFor any natural number\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nthe above identity states the following:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(n+1) = n \\Gamma(n)\n$$\n\nNow, let's repeatedly expand out the right-hand side, noticing that most of the terms can be grouped into a factorial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΓ(n + 1) &= nΓ(n) \\\\\n&= n \\cdot (n - 1) \\cdot Γ(n - 1)^(⏞)^(Γ(n)) \\\\\n&= n \\cdot (n - 1) \\cdot (n - 2) \\cdot Γ(n - 2)^(⏞)^(Γ(n - 1)) \\\\\n&= … \\\\\n&= n \\cdot (n - 1) \\cdot (n - 2) \\cdot … \\cdot 1_(⏟)_(n!) \\cdot Γ(1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(n+1) = n! \\cdot \\Gamma(1)\n$$\n\nTo evaluate $\\Gamma(1)$, we can use the definition of the gamma function:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(1) = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-t} \\, \\textrm dt = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(n+1) = n\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "66ce2df9-01d6-5873-b9e6-bb9e2a7d35af" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5339 :tutorial/title "Proving the Relationship Between the Gamma Function and the Factorial"} :step/id #uuid "272794b0-bed3-5ee3-a248-3dc313b1fd31" :step/math-academy-id 33412 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172951}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172951 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173182 :tutorial/content "Previously, we used the fact that\n\n$$\n\\Gamma \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) = \\sqrt{\\pi}\n$$\n\nTo prove this, we can directly compute the value of\n\n$$\n\\Gamma \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)\n$$\n\nusing the definition of the gamma function:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = \\int_0^\\infty t^{-1/2} e^{-t} \\, \\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe simplify this integral using the substitution $t = u^2$, which implies\n\n$$\n\\text{d}t = 2u \\, \\text{d}u\n$$\n\nAlso, note that $u \\to 0^+$ as $t \\to 0^+$ and $u \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty$. So, we have\n\n$$\nt^{-1/2} e^{-t} \\, \\text{d}t = (u^2)^{-1/2} e^{-u^2} \\cdot 2u \\, \\text{d}u = e^{-u^2} \\cdot 2 \\, \\text{d}u\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\Gamma\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = \\int_0^\\infty t^{-1/2} e^{-t} \\, \\text{d}t = 2 \\int_0^\\infty e^{-u^2} \\, \\text{d}u\n$$\n\nThis is directly related to the well-known **Gaussian integral**:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty e^{-u^2} \\, \\text{d}u = \\sqrt{\\pi}\n$$\n\nSince the integrand $e^{-u^2}$ is even (i.e., symmetric about the $y$-axis), we have\n\n$$\n\\int_0^\\infty e^{-u^2} \\, \\text{d}u = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty e^{-u^2} \\, \\text{d}u = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\sqrt{\\pi}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into our expression for the Gamma function, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\Gamma\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = 2 \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2} \\sqrt{\\pi} = \\sqrt{\\pi}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "79026717-bbea-524a-a1e0-01ef7570bfac" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5340 :tutorial/title "Evaluating the Gamma Function at One-Half"} :step/id #uuid "7a3ab1f8-c83c-5b32-87fd-ef21fb859957" :step/math-academy-id 33413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172952 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172066 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171872 :question/id #uuid "aab1e422-0ac4-59b7-bed9-5ce75b4fe22d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9033"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e75041c-d0be-5db2-a0db-7c2c2b57b44e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048115}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172236}] :knowledge-point/title "Defining the Gamma Function"} :step/id #uuid "09c6442a-deed-570b-8939-d804e74acc5c" :step/math-academy-id 33743 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172947}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173037 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173224 :tutorial/content "Here, we'll prove the recursive formula for the gamma function:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(z+1) = z \\Gamma(z)\n$$\n\nThe formula comes from applying integration by parts to the integral in the definition of the gamma function. By definition,\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(z) = \\int_0^\\infty t^{z-1} e^{-t} \\, \\textrm dt, \\qquad\\textrm{Re}(z) > 0\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΓ(z + 1) &= \\int_{0}^{∞}t^{z}e^{-t}dt \\\\\n&= \\left.- t^{z}e^{-t}\\right|_{0}^{∞} - \\int_{0}^{∞} - zt^{z - 1}e^{-t}dt \\\\\n&= lim_(t → ∞)(-\\frac{t^{z}}{e^{t}}) - \\left.(-\\frac{t^{z}}{e^{t}})\\right|_{t = 0} + \\int_{0}^{∞}zt^{z - 1}e^{-t}dt \\\\\n&= 0 - 0 + z\\int_{0}^{∞}t^{z - 1}e^{-t}dt_(⏟)_(Γ(z)) \\\\\n&= zΓ(z)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "68b4463b-d790-5bf1-9972-da35d3818a8b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6699 :tutorial/title "Proof of the Recursive Formula"} :step/id #uuid "b3e9fad8-09f6-5769-9d53-ab348d94359e" :step/math-academy-id 40566 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172950}}] :activity/title "The Gamma Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172142}]}
